  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nRest of Today and Monday: Dry with Mild Temperatures\n\nWesterly flow aloft is forecast to prevail today through Monday \nacross the Upper Midwest. In the wake of the frontal passage this \nmorning, drier air has moved in over the region, especially at the \nsurface. Even with cooling temperatures at H850 today, the afternoon \nhighs should be able to reach the upper 70s to low 80s with the \nsurface wind out of the northwest. Given the somewhat drier air at \nthe surface, temperatures tonight should be able to drop into the \nlower 60s to 50s. Light wind and a shallow moist layer at the \nsurface may lead to fog across portions of the CWA on Monday \nmorning, especially for areas in Wisconsin. Very similar conditions \nare expected on Monday with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s with \nlight winds.\n\nMonday Night through Wednesday Morning: Shower and Thunderstorm \nPotential\n\nMid level moisture moves back over the Upper Mississippi River \nValley late Monday afternoon through Tuesday. As this occurs, a \nshortwave embedded in the westerly flow aloft will move over the \nregion. The combination of these features should lead to scattered \nshower and thunderstorm development. Severe thunderstorms are not \nanticipated with this activity since forecast soundings only depict \na few hundred joules of CAPE throughout the atmospheric profiles. \nAny shower or thunderstorm activity that does develop and move \neast/southeast over the area should dissipate on Tuesday morning \nacross western into central Wisconsin.\n\nAfter a brief break in the rain chances mid day on Tuesday, another \nround will be possible on Tuesday afternoon into Wednesday morning \nas the base of the H300 trough digs south over the Midwest. \nUnfortunately for those across northern Wisconsin and the northern \nportions of the forecast area, the better chances (40-70 percent) \nfor rain continue to trend towards the southern half of the area and \nmoreso into Iowa. The latest 16/13z NBM continues to show this trend \nin the probability for 1 inch of rain or more, which now shows the \nhighest chance is only upwards of 30 percent across northeastern \nIowa. That percentage is down from 45-50 percent that the NBM had \nyesterday at this time. Even though the heavier rain potential \ncontinues to shift south, the potential for a storm or two become \nsevere remains across mainly northeastern Iowa into southwestern \nWisconsin on Tuesday afternoon into the early evening. Confidence is \nquite low in that potential at this time, especially given \ninconsistencies in the model guidance as to where the better lift \nprovided by the shortwave will actually move over. Showers and \nstorms may linger into early Wednesday morning across the far south. \n\nThursday to Next Weekend: Periodic Rain Chances with Mild Conditions\n\nNorthwest flow aloft is forecast beyond Wednesday as troughing \nremains over the eastern CONUS with a ridge over the Intermountain \nWest. This pattern will likely allow shortwaves to move over the \narea during this time frame leading to periods of showers and \nstorms, but timing differences leads to low confidence in where and \nwhen any rain occurs at this time. Temperatures should remain around \nto slightly below average going into next weekend.",
